---
source: tests/disambiguate.rs
expression: "render(&txtql::ambiguity_reports(&o.ambiguities, \"input\", input))"
---
txtql::input::ambiguous

  ⚠ ambiguous match in rule `TEXT`: the text can be read in two ways with different results
   ╭─[input:1:1]
 1 │ 1234
   · ─┬─┬
   ·  │ ╰── alternative: repetition = "12"
   ·  ╰── chosen: repetition = "123"
   ╰────
  help: txtql picked the first reading, which gives {"first":["1","2","3"],"rest":["4"]}; the
        other gives {"first":["1","2"],"rest":["3","4"]}.
        Make the pattern more specific (literals, SPLITBY, LAZY) if the other reading was
        intended.
